    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Executive summary",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-executive-summary.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Background\nDigital health, or the use of digital technologies for health, has become a salient field of practice \nfor employing routine and innovative forms of information and communications technology \n(ICT) to address health needs. The term digital health is rooted in eHealth, which is defined as \n“the use of information and communications technology in support of health and health-related \nfields”. Mobile health (mHealth) is a subset of eHealth and is defined as “the use of mobile wireless \ntechnologies for health”. More recently, the term digital health was introduced as “a broad umbrella",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The executive summary explains the terms digital health, eHealth and mHealth and the 2018 World Health Assembly resolution, and warns against rolling out digital tools without evidence. It uses an adapted Tanahashi framework to link digital interventions to health system gaps in accountability, supply, demand, quality and affordability. It sets out the guideline's aims and stresses that digital tools do not replace functioning health systems. The end shows how ten recommendations, from birth and death notification to telemedicine, decision support and training content, fit across the health system.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
