    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 25,
      "pageEnd": 26,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-11-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Background\nDigital health, or the use of digital technologies for health, has become a salient field of practice \nfor employing routine and innovative forms of information and communications technology (ICT) \nto address health needs.  The term digital health is rooted in eHealth, which is defined as “the use \nof information and communications technology in support of health and health-related fields” (1). \nMobile health (mHealth) is a subset of eHealth and is defined as “the use of mobile wireless \ntechnologies for public health” (2,3). More recently, the term digital health was introduced as",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "stale",
        "text": "The background defines digital health, eHealth and mHealth and notes over a thousand recorded digital health deployments since 2008. It recalls the 2018 Assembly resolution and warns that many tools are rolled out without evidence of benefit, so their effects must be evaluated.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Role of digital health in health system strengthening and universal health coverage",
      "pageStart": 26,
      "pageEnd": 31,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-12-role-of-digital-health-in-health-system-stren.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "strengthening and universal health coverage\nUHC aims to ensure the quality, accessibility and affordability of health services. However, \nshortfalls remain in ensuring access to all who need health services and in ensuring that they are \ndelivered with the intended quality without causing financial hardship to the people accessing \nthem (9). The Tanahashi framework published by WHO in 1978 provides a time-tested model of \nunderstanding health system performance gaps and how they prevent the intended coverage, \nquality and affordability of health services to individuals (10). This cascading mod",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Objectives of this guideline",
      "pageStart": 31,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-13-objectives-of-this-guideline.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Objectives of this guideline\nThis guideline responds to the 2018 World Health Assembly Resolution on Digital Health, \nrequesting WHO to provide Member States with normative guidance to inform the adoption of \nevidence-based digital health interventions. Within the Resolution, Member States specifically \nrequest: \n … that WHO builds on its strengths, by developing guidance for digital health, including, but not limited to, health \ndata protection and usage, on the basis of its existing guidelines and successful examples from global, regional and \nnational programmes, including through the ident",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that the guideline responds to the 2018 Assembly resolution by evaluating evidence on digital health interventions, including benefits, harms, feasibility and equity. It stresses that digital tools complement but do not replace core health system components, and lists the interventions reviewed, from birth notification to training content for health workers.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Target audience",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-14-target-audience.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Target audience\nThe primary target audience for this guideline is decision-makers in ministries of health and \npublic health practitioners, to aid them to develop a better understanding of which digital health \ninterventions have an evidence base to address health system needs. This guideline may also prove \nbeneficial to organizations that invest resources into digital health systems as implementation \nand development partners. This document aims to strengthen evidence-based decision-making on \ndigital approaches by governments and partner institutions, encouraging the mainstreaming and \ninst",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Names ministry of health decision-makers and public health practitioners as the main audience, with development partners also served, and notes that recommended interventions can be used on any digital device.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Linkages with other WHO resources",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-15-linkages-with-other-who-resources.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Linkages with other WHO resources\nWHO has published several resources on digital health, yet to date has not released normative \nguidelines detailing recommendations about which digital health interventions are supported by \ndemonstrable evidence for addressing specific health system challenges. \nSeveral WHO clinical and public health guidelines have been developed that include \nrecommendations for digital technologies alongside other interventions, such as medication \nadherence and supporting community health workers. These include:\nȺȺ 2016 Consolidated guidelines on the use of antiretroviral",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that WHO has not yet issued guidelines on which digital interventions have evidence behind them, though some clinical guidelines include digital tools. It describes related WHO resources such as the National eHealth Strategy Toolkit, the Global Observatory for eHealth, the classification of digital interventions, the Digital Health Atlas and Be He@lthy, Be Mobile.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Context and the enabling environment",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 35,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-16-context-and-the-enabling-environment.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Context and the enabling environment\nThe maturity of the ecosystem, comprising the enabling and ICT environments, has a critical \ninfluence on the relevance and impact of the recommended digital health interventions. The \nenabling environment is defined as the attitudes, actions, policies and practices that support \nthe effective and efficient functioning of organizations and programmes. For digital health, \nthis includes factors such as the leadership, governance mechanisms, regulatory and policy \nframeworks, strategy and financial investment, workforce capacity, standards and interoperabilit",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the maturity of the enabling and ICT environments strongly influences how relevant and effective the recommended digital interventions will be, defining both terms. It advises assessing ecosystem readiness and, where it is weak, strengthening the health system and the enabling environment before implementation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Linkages to broader digital health architecture",
      "pageStart": 35,
      "pageEnd": 36,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-17-linkages-to-broader-digital-health-architectu.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "architecture\nDigital health interventions are intended to integrate with and fit into an overall digital health \narchitecture. The digital health architecture provides an overview or blueprint to describe how \ndifferent digital applications (software and ICT systems) and related functionalities would interact \nwith each other within a given context (25). While the unit of analysis for this guideline focuses on \nthe value of specific digital interventions, there is an equally important need to support a cohesive \napproach to implementation, in which different digital interventions can operate t",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Stresses that digital health interventions should fit into a wider digital health architecture so that systems work together rather than as isolated duplicates. It points stakeholders to complementary guidance, such as the WHO/ITU National eHealth Strategy Toolkit and the ITU Digital Health Platform Handbook, and lists related networks and communities of practice.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Living guidelines approach",
      "pageStart": 36,
      "pageEnd": 37,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-18-living-guidelines-approach.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Living guidelines approach\nThis guideline includes recommendations on a list of prioritized digital health interventions \naccessible via mobile devices, representing a subset of a much larger set of digital interventions. \nThis guideline aims to incorporate a broader set of emerging digital health interventions gradually \nin subsequent versions. The WHO Classification of digital health interventions v1.0 (13), provides \na starting point to tackle the evolving nature of digital health and to identify interventions for \nfuture inclusion in updated guidelines. This version applies WHO Guidelines",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the \"living guidelines\" approach, under which the guideline covers a prioritised subset of mobile-accessible digital health interventions and will add further interventions and health areas as evidence emerges. It gives targeted client communication for noncommunicable diseases as an example of a planned extension.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Methods",
      "pageStart": 37,
      "pageEnd": 37,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-2-methods.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The development of this guideline followed the methods described in the second edition of the \nWHO handbook for guideline development (35). This institution-wide process at WHO entailed \nthe identification of critical questions and outcomes, retrieval of the evidence, assessment \nand synthesis of that evidence, the formulation of recommendations, and planning for the \nimplementation, dissemination, impact evaluation and updating of this guideline. \nThe guideline development process also included two rounds of online surveys and three in-person \nconsultations. These consultations included (i) a",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Outlines how the guideline was developed under the WHO handbook for guideline development, through two online surveys and three in-person meetings held between 2016 and 2018.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Identification of priority questions",
      "pageStart": 37,
      "pageEnd": 38,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-21-identification-of-priority-questions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Identification of priority questions\nProcess for defining the scope of interventions and outcomes\nThe initial advisory meeting in February 2016 was used to explore the strategic direction of this \nguideline, including defining the objectives and the framing of digital health interventions. Since \nthere were no preceding WHO guidelines with defined terminologies for specific digital health \ninterventions, this meeting examined frameworks and standardized classifications that could be \nleveraged for the formulation of the priority questions. These included the WHO Classification \nof digital heal",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes how priority questions were identified, starting from a 2016 advisory meeting that used the WHO Classification of digital health interventions and framed questions in PICO format. It explains how the questions were refined through virtual and scoping meetings and online surveys that rated outcomes, with over 300 respondents across WHO regions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Scoping of interventions and outcomes",
      "pageStart": 38,
      "pageEnd": 45,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-22-scoping-of-interventions-and-outcomes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The scoping process focused on the following digital health interventions that were prioritized for \nevidence review (see Annex 2 for the questions in the PICO format):\nȺȺ birth notification\nȺȺ death notification\nȺȺ stock notification and commodity management\nȺȺ client1-to-provider telemedicine\nȺȺ provider-to-provider telemedicine\nȺȺ targeted client communication (spread across five population groups)\nȺȺ health worker decision support\nȺȺ tracking of patients’/clients’ health status and services\nȺȺ provision of educational and training content to health workers.\n1\t\nAlthough WHO’s Classification",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the digital health interventions prioritised for evidence review, including birth and death notification, stock management, client-to-provider and provider-to-provider telemedicine, targeted client communication, decision support, digital tracking and digital training of health workers. A table defines each intervention with its synonyms and descriptors. The section also sets out the health areas and outcomes considered for each intervention. It notes that the reviews required a mobile component but that the recommendations may also apply to non-mobile devices such as desktop computers.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Evidence retrieval",
      "pageStart": 45,
      "pageEnd": 47,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-23-evidence-retrieval.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "linkages to non-mobile digital systems. For example, the review on stock management included \ninformation on the linkages to web-based data dashboards accessed on desktop computers for \nvisualizing the data at district and national levels. \nLastly, although the systematic reviews included accessibility via mobile devices to ensure \nthat these digital interventions are applicable in low resource settings where extensive \ncomputerized systems may not be available, it does not preclude the recommendations from \nbeing used on non-mobile digital devices, such as desktop computers.\nDelivery channels",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that no restrictions were placed on delivery channels and describes the two main types of evidence used: effectiveness studies such as randomised trials and controlled before-and-after studies, and qualitative studies on acceptability, feasibility and implementation. It notes that evidence on resource use and cost-effectiveness came from what the reviews found. An overarching analysis drew together qualitative reviews, an overview of systematic reviews and mixed-methods analyses.",
        "state": "draft",
