    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-023",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Evidence and recommendations",
      "pageStart": 57,
      "pageEnd": 57,
      "target": "../sections/sec-023-3-evidence-and-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "recommendations\nThis guideline provides ten evidence-based recommendations on the digital health \ninterventions that were prioritized during the scoping process (see sections 2.1 and 2.2). \nThese recommendations are made with the expectation that their implementation is grounded \nin an understanding of the ecosystem readiness and maturity, as outlined in Chapter 4. For each of \nthe digital health interventions reviewed in this guideline, this chapter elaborates on the following \ncomponents:\nȺȺ background information on the specific digital health intervention\nȺȺ an overview of the specific evi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the chapter presenting ten evidence-based recommendations on the prioritised digital health interventions, listing what is given for each: background, evidence, recommendation with justification and implementation considerations. It refers to annexes and web supplements for evidence gaps and evidence-to-decision frameworks.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-024",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Cross-cutting acceptability and feasibility findings",
      "pageStart": 57,
      "pageEnd": 62,
      "target": "../sections/sec-024-31-cross-cutting-acceptability-and-feasibility-f.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "feasibility findings \nMost of the digital health interventions in this guideline are targeted at or expected to be used \nby health workers. The following findings point to factors that influence the acceptability and \nfeasibility of digital interventions used by health workers. These findings are based on qualitative \nAlthough the systematic reviews included accessibility via mobile devices to ensure that these digital \ninterventions are applicable in low resource settings where extensive computerized systems may not be \navailable, it does not preclude the recommended interventions from being",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises cross-cutting qualitative findings on how acceptable and feasible health workers find digital interventions. Factors that may increase acceptability include the chance for health workers to take on new and more senior tasks, while concerns include extra workload and the cost of using personal phones. Feasibility issues such as connectivity, power, training and device costs are also discussed. The section ends with findings on clients, including privacy worries among people with stigmatised conditions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-025",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Accountability coverage: Birth and death notification",
      "pageStart": 62,
      "pageEnd": 68,
      "target": "../sections/sec-025-32-accountability-coverage-birth-and-death-notif.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "birth and death notification\nBackground \nA global scale-up plan for strengthening civil registration and vital statistics (CRVS) systems has \nbeen developed by the World Bank and WHO with the goal of achieving “universal civil registration \nof births, deaths and other vital events, including reporting cause of death, and access to legal \nproof of registration for all individuals by 2030” (57). A key component of this plan is to prioritize \nand strengthen the linkages between CRVS systems and health (57–59). This includes the use \nof digital information systems to strengthen CRVS systems and ex",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers digital birth and death notification, linking it to the global plan to strengthen civil registration and vital statistics systems and the spread of mobile phones in resource-limited settings. It summarises evidence on effectiveness, acceptability and feasibility, including community sensitivities around stillbirths and maternal deaths and the need for local staffing. WHO recommends digital birth notification only where notifications provide individual-level data and the health or civil registration system can respond. Implementation considerations include data validation, health worker reporting targets and whether registration systems can absorb more notifications.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-026",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Availability of commodities: Stock notification and commodity",
      "pageStart": 68,
      "pageEnd": 72,
      "target": "../sections/sec-026-33-availability-of-commodities-stock-notificatio.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "stock notification and commodity \nBackground \nThe availability of health commodities at point of services is critical to strengthening the quality \nof care and supporting the pillars of universal health coverage (UHC) (64). Health commodities \ninclude health products, and health and medical supplies that may be needed for the provision of \nhealth services, including medicines, vaccines, medical supplies such as contraceptives dressings, \nneedles and syringes, and laboratory/diagnostic consumables (65,66). Various high-level initiatives, \nincluding the UN Commission on Life-Saving Commodities f",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers digital stock notification and commodity management, explaining why stock-outs persist, especially in rural areas, and how mobile tools such as text messaging and dashboards can help track supplies. WHO recommends this intervention only where supply chain systems can respond promptly and appropriately to notifications, despite limited evidence on effectiveness. Implementation considerations are listed against the WHO/ITU eHealth strategy toolkit framework.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-027",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Client-to-provider telemedicine",
      "pageStart": 72,
      "pageEnd": 76,
      "target": "../sections/sec-027-client-to-provider-telemedicine.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Background \nDespite progress in addressing health workforce shortages, challenges in the equitable access to \nhealth workers serves as a major hindrance to achieving the full requirements of effective coverage \nof human resources for health (70). Geographical inaccessibility and the preference of health \nworkers for working in urban environments are among some of the well-documented reasons for \nimbalances in the distribution of health workers (71). While there is a wide range of ongoing efforts \nto reduce inequities in access to health workers, including incentives and alternative approaches",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers client-to-provider telemedicine as a way to overcome geographic barriers and uneven distribution of health workers, defining telemedicine and its forms. It summarises evidence on effects, acceptability and feasibility, including access issues for people with hearing impairments or low digital literacy. WHO recommends it only as a complement to face-to-face care in settings able to monitor safety, privacy, traceability, accountability and security for patients, followed by implementation considerations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-028",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Provider-to-provider telemedicine",
      "pageStart": 76,
      "pageEnd": 80,
      "target": "../sections/sec-028-provider-to-provider-telemedicine.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Background \nAccess to qualified health workers with the appropriate competencies, skills and behaviours is an \neven greater obstacle to improving health outcomes than the availability of health workers (70,71). \nGeographical inaccessibility and the unequal distribution of health workers also contribute to \nlimitations in the effective coverage of human resources for health (62). Digital approaches, most \nnotably telemedicine between different types of health workers, have emerged as a potential \nway to overcome the barriers of long distances to qualified health workers and shortages in their",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "stale",
        "text": "Covers provider-to-provider telemedicine, which links less skilled health workers with specialists for diagnosis, remote monitoring and case management, either in real time or by store-and-forward exchange. It summarises the evidence on effects, acceptability and feasibility. WHO recommends it where safety, privacy, traceability, accountability and security for patients can be monitored, including procedures for consent, data protection and checking health worker credentials, followed by implementation considerations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-029",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Contact and continuous coverage: targeted client communication for behaviour change related to sexual, reproductive, maternal, newborn, child and adolescent health",
      "pageStart": 80,
      "pageEnd": 87,
      "target": "../sections/sec-029-36-contact-and-continuous-coverage-targeted-clie.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "targeted client communication \nfor behaviour change \nrelated to sexual, reproductive, maternal, newborn, \nchild and adolescent health\nBackground \nTargeted client communication2 – defined as the transmission of health content or information \nto a specific audience based on their health status or demographic profile (13) – represents an \napproach for engaging with individuals to increase their knowledge about health and health-\nseeking behaviours, about where to find or how to access services, or for helping to retain \nthem within health services when follow-up is needed. This includes the trans",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-030",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Effective coverage: Health worker decision support",
      "pageStart": 87,
      "pageEnd": 91,
      "target": "../sections/sec-030-37-effective-coverage-health-worker-decision-sup.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Health worker decision support\nBackground \nQuality of care, defined as the “degree to which health services for individuals and populations \nincrease the likelihood of desired health outcomes and are consistent with current professional \nknowledge”, is a foundational component of universal health coverage (76). Quality of care has \nconsistently been documented as suboptimal, particularly across low- and middle-income \ncountries. Commonly cited reasons for poor quality of care have included health workers’ \ninaccurate diagnosis, inappropriate or unnecessary treatment, inadequate or unsafe clini",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers the guideline section on decision support tools for health workers, starting from the definition of quality of care and the reasons it is often poor in low- and middle-income countries. It reviews how digital decision support aims to improve health workers' adherence to recommended practice and the evidence and considerations behind the recommendation. It notes that such tools are often integrated with digital health records and points to the section on combining them with digital tracking.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-031",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Multiple points of coverage: Digital tracking of clients’ health status and services combined with decision-support and targeted client communication",
      "pageStart": 91,
      "pageEnd": 97,
      "target": "../sections/sec-031-38-multiple-points-of-coverage-digital-tracking.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "digital tracking of clients’ \nhealth status and services \ncombined with decision-support and \ntargeted client communication\nBackground \nThe use of paper-based systems in the delivery of health services introduces a clerical burden \non health workers. Additionally, the ability for health workers to keep track of clients effectively, \nand follow up on services, whether within the facility or in the community, is essential to the \ncontinuity of care (12).\nDigital tracking is the use of a digitized record to capture and store health information on clients in \norder to follow-up on their health sta",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "stale",
        "text": "Covers the guideline section on digital tracking of clients' health status and services combined with decision support and targeted client communication. It explains how digital registers and electronic records reduce the clerical burden of paper systems and support follow-up and continuity of care. It sets out the evidence and conditions for the recommendation, including concerns about privacy and data security. It closes by urging that local policies on digital identity be considered so that programmes do no harm.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-032",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Effective coverage: digital provision of training and educational content to health workers",
      "pageStart": 97,
      "pageEnd": 101,
      "target": "../sections/sec-032-39-effective-coverage-digital-provision-of-train.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "digital provision of training \nand educational content \nto health workers\nBackground \nBroadly defined as the management and provision of educational and training content in digital \nform for health professionals, electronic learning (eLearning) has emerged as one approach to \nincreasing health workers’ access to training and educational resources (18). More recently, the \nwidespread reach of mobile devices has prompted the use of such technologies to deliver training \ncontent to health workers, also known as mobile learning (mLearning). Such training content may \nbe exchanged using channels su",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-033",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Implementation considerations",
      "pageStart": 101,
      "pageEnd": 101,
      "target": "../sections/sec-033-4-implementation-considerations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "considerations\nDigital health has the potential to help address problems such as distance and access, but still \nshares many of the underlying challenges faced by health system interventions in general, \nincluding poor governance, insufficient training, infrastructural limitations, and poor access \nto equipment and supplies. These considerations need to be addressed in addition to specific \nrequirements introduced by digital health. As the context will moderate the eventual impact \nof digital health interventions, the broader health system and enabling environment become \nespecially critical.",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens the chapter on implementation considerations, noting that digital health shares the general health system challenges of governance, training, infrastructure and supplies, so the wider enabling environment is critical.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-034",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Linking the recommendations across the health system",
      "pageStart": 101,
      "pageEnd": 103,
      "target": "../sections/sec-034-41-linking-the-recommendations-across-the-health.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "across the health system\nWhile the recommendations included in this guideline are based on distinct digital interventions, \nthey all contribute to the health systems’ needs in different but interlinked ways. For health \nsystem managers, the recommendation on digital stock notification aims to drive availability of \ncommodities at the point of services.  From the clients’ and patients’ perspectives, this would \ninclude ability to access health information and services more immediately, such as through \nclient to provider telemedicine and targeted client communication. Likewise, health workers n",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
