    {
      "id": "library/10665-311941/blocks/prose-sec-039",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Considerations for the design of future evaluations",
      "pageStart": 112,
      "pageEnd": 113,
      "target": "../sections/sec-039-52-considerations-for-the-design-of-future-evalu.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "future evaluations\nThe GDG also identified several issues related to the design of future evaluations of digital health \ninterventions, including the following:\nȺȺ Health system focused digital interventions, such as stock management and birth and death \nnotification, are often complex in the number of components, behaviours targeted, and \norganizational levels involved (89). These factors may make designs such as randomized \ncontrolled trials for evaluating the effectiveness of these interventions difficult to apply. Other \ndesigns may therefore need to be considered, such as controlled befor",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the guideline group's suggestions for designing future evaluations of digital health interventions, such as alternatives to randomized trials for complex system-level interventions and attention to intermediate outcomes. It also calls for common metrics and tools for assessing effectiveness and cost-effectiveness.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
